# The Docker build context is the repository root — for BOTH service Dockerfiles
# (services/render and services/research) — so this file decides what either container is
# allowed to have a copy of.
#
# THE TWO IMAGES NEED DIFFERENT THINGS AND THIS FILE IS SHARED, so the rule it follows is:
# exclude what NEITHER needs, and let each Dockerfile's own COPY lines decide the rest. A
# path left in the context but never COPYed costs a few bytes of upload and reaches no
# image. A path excluded here that one Dockerfile COPYs is a hard build failure.
#
# THE FIRST THREE LINES ARE THE SECURITY ONES. A .env that reached the image would put
# chain keys, the LLM key and the Redis credentials inside the process that runs the
# investigated party's JavaScript — the exact thing services/render is designed to hold
# none of. .git would carry every secret ever committed and then removed.
.env
.env.*
.git
.github

# Nothing either service needs, and all of it large. Each `node_modules` is listed
# explicitly because a .dockerignore pattern without a slash matches only at the context
# root — a locally-installed services/research/node_modules would otherwise be copied over
# the one the image just built, which is how a Linux image ends up with a Windows binary in
# it.
node_modules
services/render/node_modules
services/research/node_modules
.next
out
dist
data
coverage
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

# The Next.js app. It is deployed on Vercel and has no business in either image.
#
# `config` IS NOT ON THIS LIST, and that is not an oversight. lib/stock-tokens.js and
# lib/market-evidence.js import config/stock-tokens.json at module load, so the research
# service's chain leg does not even PARSE without it — see services/research/Dockerfile,
# which COPYs it. Excluding it here would fail that build outright. It never reaches the
# render image, because services/render/Dockerfile does not copy it.
app
components
schema
scripts
test
public
next.config.mjs
postcss.config.mjs
tailwind.config.mjs
jsconfig.json
instrumentation.js
vercel.json
